#import "/shared/book.typ": *
#let p = problem.with(code: "MM5020", unit: "U3")
#let items = (
 p(1,"E","U3.1 Kernels",[Find the kernel and image of $f:ZZ -> ZZ slash 8 ZZ$, $f(n)=[3n]$.],[Three is invertible modulo eight.],[Its inverse is also three.],[The equation $3n=0$ modulo eight is equivalent to $n=0$ modulo eight, so the kernel is $8 ZZ$. Every residue $[a]$ is $f(3a)$ because $9a=a$ modulo eight; the image is the whole target.],[Give explicit reasons for both the kernel and surjectivity.]),
 p(2,"E","U3.1 Normality",[Show the kernel of the determinant map $"GL"_2(RR) -> RR^times$ is normal.],[Use the homomorphism property of determinants.],[The kernel consists of matrices of determinant one.],[$det(A B)=det(A)det(B)$, so determinant is a group homomorphism. Its kernel is $"SL"_2(RR)$. Every homomorphism kernel is normal; alternatively $det(A B A^(-1))=det(B)=1$ for $B$ in the kernel.],[Identify the kernel and cite or prove the applicable normality criterion.]),
 p(3,"E","U3.2 Quotient size",[Let $|G|=60$ and $N triangleleft G$ have order twelve. Determine $|G slash N|$ and its group structure.],[Count cosets.],[A group of order five is cyclic.],[The quotient has $60/12=5$ elements. Since five is prime, every nonidentity quotient element generates it, so $G slash N iso C_5$.],[Use normality for the quotient group and Lagrange for its size.]),
 p(4,"E","U3.1 Homomorphisms",[Show a homomorphism $f:G -> H$ satisfies $f(g^n)=f(g)^n$ for every integer $n$.],[Handle positive, zero, and negative exponents separately.],[Use preservation of inverses for negative exponents.],[Induction and multiplication preservation prove the identity for positive $n$. For $n=0$, both sides are the identity because $f(e)=e$. If $n=-m<0$, then $f(g^n)=f((g^(-1))^m)=f(g^(-1))^m=f(g)^(-m)$.],[Do not omit the negative or zero cases.]),
 p(5,"C","U3.2 First isomorphism theorem",[Identify $RR^times slash \{1,-1\}$ using a natural homomorphism.],[Take the absolute value.],[Its image is the multiplicative group of positive real numbers.],[The map $x |-> |x|$ is a surjective multiplicative homomorphism onto $RR_(>0)$ with kernel $\{1,-1\}$. The first isomorphism theorem gives the quotient isomorphism $[x] |-> |x|$.],[Specify the target operation and compute the kernel.]),
 p(6,"C","U3.1 Normality",[Prove the center $Z(G)$ is a normal subgroup.],[Check the subgroup test, then conjugation.],[Central elements are fixed by every conjugation.],[If $a,b$ commute with every $g$, then so does $a b^(-1)$, since $g a b^(-1)=a g b^(-1)=a b^(-1)g$. The identity is central, so this is a subgroup. For $z in Z(G)$, $g z g^(-1)=z$, proving normality.],[Distinguish closure from the separate conjugation argument.]),
 p(7,"C","U3.2 Quotient maps",[For positive integers $m,n$, when is $[a]_m |-> [a]_n$ well defined? When it is, find its kernel.],[The representatives zero and $m$ must agree after mapping.],[The necessary and sufficient condition is $n|m$.],[Necessity follows because $[m]_m=[0]_m$ forces $[m]_n=0$. If $n|m$, a difference divisible by $m$ is divisible by $n$, proving sufficiency. The kernel is the set of multiples of $n$ modulo $m$, namely $\{[0],[n],dots,[(m/n-1)n]\}$, of order $m/n$.],[Prove necessity and sufficiency, then list the kernel in the correct source group.]),
 p(8,"C","U3.2 Correspondence",[List all subgroups of $ZZ slash 12 ZZ$ by using subgroups of $ZZ$ containing $12 ZZ$.],[All subgroups of $ZZ$ are $d ZZ$.],[Containment $12 ZZ subset.eq d ZZ$ means $d|12$.],[The possible $d$ are $1,2,3,4,6,12$. Their images are $d ZZ slash 12 ZZ$, generated by $[d]$, with orders $12/d$. The correspondence theorem proves there are no others.],[Pay attention to the reversal between divisibility and ideal/subgroup containment.]),
 p(9,"C","U3.1 Cyclic quotient by center",[Prove that if $G slash Z(G)$ is cyclic, then $G$ is abelian.],[Express every element using a representative of the quotient generator.],[Write $x=g^r z$ and $y=g^s w$ with $z,w$ central.],[Choose $g Z(G)$ generating the quotient. Then write $x=g^r z$ and $y=g^s w$ for integers $r,s$ and $z,w in Z(G)$, since their cosets are powers of the generating coset. Since $z,w$ commute with everything, $x y=g^(r+s)z w=g^(s+r)w z=y x$. Hence $G$ is abelian.],[Explain why the expressions cover all elements and why the central factors may move.]),
 p(10,"A","U3.2 Universal property",[Prove that a homomorphism $f:G -> H$ factors through $G slash N$ exactly when $N subset.eq ker f$, and prove uniqueness of the factor.],[A factor must kill the kernel of the projection.],[For sufficiency define $overline(f)(g N)=f(g)$ and check representatives.],[If $f=overline(f)pi$, then $n in N$ maps to $overline(f)(N)=e$, so $N subset.eq ker f$. Conversely the containment makes equal cosets have equal images, because $b^(-1)a in N$ implies $f(a)=f(b)$. The candidate respects products by the homomorphism law. Every coset is $pi(g)$, forcing any factor's value there to be $f(g)$, so it is unique.],[Give both directions and all well-definedness details.]),
 p(11,"A","U3.2 Abelianization",[Let $[G,G]$ be generated by all commutators $a b a^(-1)b^(-1)$. Show it is normal, its quotient is abelian, and every map from $G$ to an abelian group factors uniquely through this quotient.],[Conjugation takes commutators to commutators.],[An abelian target kills each commutator.],[Conjugating a commutator by $g$ gives the commutator of $g a g^(-1)$ and $g b g^(-1)$, so conjugation preserves the generated subgroup. In the quotient each commutator is the identity, precisely the equation making the cosets of $a,b$ commute. If $f$ has abelian target, the image of every commutator is the identity, so $[G,G] subset.eq ker f$. The quotient universal property gives the unique factorization.],[Show normality for the generated subgroup, not only for individual generators.]),
 p(12,"A","U3.1 Failure of converse to Lagrange",[Prove that $A_4$ has no subgroup of order six. You may use that three-cycles generate $A_4$.],[A subgroup of order six would have index two.],[The quotient would have order two, but every three-cycle must map to its identity.],[Such a subgroup would be normal by the index-two theorem, yielding a surjective homomorphism $A_4 -> C_2$. The image of an element of order three has order dividing both three and two, hence is the identity. Since three-cycles generate $A_4$, the homomorphism would be trivial, contradicting surjectivity.],[Explain why the image order divides the original order and why generators determine the map.]),
)
